waves of the blue seas of the caribbean  roll onto the white sands and palm  tree-lined beaches  of the northern honduran coast  the small communities who inhabit this  biodiverse paradise  survive predominantly on the fish they  take from the waters  and the vegetables they grow  it looks like paradise but its beauty  and its location  has turned deadly for these people  the garifuna and afro-caribbean  population originally from the island of  saint vincent  who were evicted from their homes by the  british in the 1700s  have lived here and protected this land  for generations  but drug cartels palm oil manufacturers  and tourism developers want to take it  from them  and they're prepared to kill to do it  last summer for garafuna and a visitor  were abducted by armed men from a series  of houses in the village  amongst them the community's leader they  say  he was tortured at this house before  they were all taken away  none have been seen again it's assumed  they're dead  cesar now leads the community he told me  that drug cartels want the garifuna's  remote beaches to ship drugs to the  united states  while property developers want the  beachfronts and manufacturers their palm  oil  um  companies  my  these people are poor and are clearly  ill-equipped to take on drug gangs  developers or businessmen  the inter-american human rights  commission ruled in 2015 that all the  land taken from the garifuna  has to be returned to them fact is  hardly any has been  and crucially none that's on the  beachfront and of course that is where  developers want to build hotels on land  that these people consider sacred  [Music]  it may have been hundreds of years ago  but being evicted once has left this  community determined to make sure it  doesn't happen again  surviving off the land and the sea is  just about possible  but a sustained attack on their natural  resources their environment  and their people could see this  vulnerable group  shattered once more mr ramsey sky news  honduras
 

